<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8" standalone="yes"?><rss version="2.0" xmlns:atom="http://www.w3.org/2005/Atom"><channel><title>Conditionals on iOS UIKit Course</title><link>/docs/02-swift-fundamentals/conditionals/</link><description>Recent content in Conditionals on iOS UIKit Course</description><generator>Hugo</generator><language>en-us</language><atom:link href="/docs/02-swift-fundamentals/conditionals/index.xml" rel="self" type="application/rss+xml"/><item><title>4.1. If statements</title><link>/docs/02-swift-fundamentals/conditionals/4.1.-if-statements/</link><pubDate>Mon, 01 Jan 0001 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><guid>/docs/02-swift-fundamentals/conditionals/4.1.-if-statements/</guid><description>&lt;h1 id="41-if-statements"&gt;4.1. If statements&lt;a class="anchor" href="#41-if-statements"&gt;#&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/h1&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;code&gt;if&lt;/code&gt; statements&lt;/strong&gt; are the simplest type of conditional. They allow us to specify what should happen if a condition is &lt;code&gt;true&lt;/code&gt;. For example, the following code prints &amp;ldquo;It is 5!&amp;rdquo; if the variable &lt;code&gt;myNum&lt;/code&gt; is equal to 5:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;div class="highlight"&gt;&lt;pre tabindex="0" style="color:#f8f8f2;background-color:#272822;-moz-tab-size:4;-o-tab-size:4;tab-size:4;-webkit-text-size-adjust:none;"&gt;&lt;code class="language-swift" data-lang="swift"&gt;&lt;span style="display:flex;"&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;span style="color:#66d9ef"&gt;let&lt;/span&gt; myNum = &lt;span style="color:#ae81ff"&gt;5&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="display:flex;"&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;span style="color:#75715e"&gt;//condition myNum==5 returns either true or false...&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="display:flex;"&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;span style="color:#66d9ef"&gt;if&lt;/span&gt; myNum == &lt;span style="color:#ae81ff"&gt;5&lt;/span&gt; {
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="display:flex;"&gt;&lt;span&gt; &lt;span style="color:#75715e"&gt;//This code block executes if true...&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="display:flex;"&gt;&lt;span&gt; print(&lt;span style="color:#e6db74"&gt;&amp;#34;It is 5!&amp;#34;&lt;/span&gt;)
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="display:flex;"&gt;&lt;span&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&lt;/div&gt;</description></item><item><title>4.2. If-else statements</title><link>/docs/02-swift-fundamentals/conditionals/4.2.-if-else-statements/</link><pubDate>Mon, 01 Jan 0001 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><guid>/docs/02-swift-fundamentals/conditionals/4.2.-if-else-statements/</guid><description>&lt;h1 id="42-if-else-statements"&gt;4.2. If-else statements&lt;a class="anchor" href="#42-if-else-statements"&gt;#&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/h1&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;code&gt;if-else&lt;/code&gt; statements&lt;/strong&gt; allow us to specify what should happen if a condition is &lt;code&gt;true&lt;/code&gt; or &lt;code&gt;false&lt;/code&gt;. So, now we can add a code block if the condition is &lt;code&gt;false&lt;/code&gt;. We can modify our previous example to print &amp;ldquo;It is not 5!&amp;rdquo;, if &lt;code&gt;myNum&lt;/code&gt; is not 5.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;div class="highlight"&gt;&lt;pre tabindex="0" style="color:#f8f8f2;background-color:#272822;-moz-tab-size:4;-o-tab-size:4;tab-size:4;-webkit-text-size-adjust:none;"&gt;&lt;code class="language-swift" data-lang="swift"&gt;&lt;span style="display:flex;"&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;span style="color:#66d9ef"&gt;if&lt;/span&gt; myNum == &lt;span style="color:#ae81ff"&gt;5&lt;/span&gt; {
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="display:flex;"&gt;&lt;span&gt; print(&lt;span style="color:#e6db74"&gt;&amp;#34;It is 5!&amp;#34;&lt;/span&gt;)
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="display:flex;"&gt;&lt;span&gt;}&lt;span style="color:#66d9ef"&gt;else&lt;/span&gt;{
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="display:flex;"&gt;&lt;span&gt; print(&lt;span style="color:#e6db74"&gt;&amp;#34;It is not 5!&amp;#34;&lt;/span&gt;)
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="display:flex;"&gt;&lt;span&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;p&gt;You can build a &lt;code&gt;if-else if-else&lt;/code&gt; chain if we are handling multiple &lt;code&gt;true&lt;/code&gt; conditions. For example, the following code prints &lt;code&gt;myNum&lt;/code&gt; if &lt;code&gt;myNum&lt;/code&gt; is 1, 3, or 5. Else it prints &amp;ldquo;Not 1, 3, or 5!&amp;rdquo;&lt;/p&gt;</description></item><item><title>4.3. Switch statements</title><link>/docs/02-swift-fundamentals/conditionals/4.3.-switch-statements/</link><pubDate>Mon, 01 Jan 0001 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><guid>/docs/02-swift-fundamentals/conditionals/4.3.-switch-statements/</guid><description>&lt;h1 id="43-switch-statements"&gt;4.3. Switch statements&lt;a class="anchor" href="#43-switch-statements"&gt;#&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/h1&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Instead of &lt;code&gt;if-else if-else&lt;/code&gt; chain, we can use &lt;code&gt;switch&lt;/code&gt; statements if multiple statements are &lt;code&gt;true&lt;/code&gt;. For example, the following code prints the name of the day of the week, depending on the value of the constant &lt;code&gt;day&lt;/code&gt;. If the value of &lt;code&gt;day&lt;/code&gt; is 1 through 7, the code prints the name of the day; else, it prints &amp;ldquo;Invalid!&amp;rdquo;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;div class="highlight"&gt;&lt;pre tabindex="0" style="color:#f8f8f2;background-color:#272822;-moz-tab-size:4;-o-tab-size:4;tab-size:4;-webkit-text-size-adjust:none;"&gt;&lt;code class="language-swift" data-lang="swift"&gt;&lt;span style="display:flex;"&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;span style="color:#75715e"&gt;//switch statements...&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="display:flex;"&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;span style="color:#66d9ef"&gt;let&lt;/span&gt; day = &lt;span style="color:#ae81ff"&gt;5&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="display:flex;"&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;span style="color:#66d9ef"&gt;switch&lt;/span&gt; day {
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="display:flex;"&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;span style="color:#66d9ef"&gt;case&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span style="color:#ae81ff"&gt;1&lt;/span&gt;:
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="display:flex;"&gt;&lt;span&gt; print(&lt;span style="color:#e6db74"&gt;&amp;#34;Sunday&amp;#34;&lt;/span&gt;)
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="display:flex;"&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;span style="color:#66d9ef"&gt;case&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span style="color:#ae81ff"&gt;2&lt;/span&gt;:
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="display:flex;"&gt;&lt;span&gt; print(&lt;span style="color:#e6db74"&gt;&amp;#34;Monday&amp;#34;&lt;/span&gt;)
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="display:flex;"&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;span style="color:#66d9ef"&gt;case&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span style="color:#ae81ff"&gt;3&lt;/span&gt;:
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="display:flex;"&gt;&lt;span&gt; print(&lt;span style="color:#e6db74"&gt;&amp;#34;Tuesday&amp;#34;&lt;/span&gt;)
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="display:flex;"&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;span style="color:#66d9ef"&gt;case&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span style="color:#ae81ff"&gt;4&lt;/span&gt;:
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="display:flex;"&gt;&lt;span&gt; print(&lt;span style="color:#e6db74"&gt;&amp;#34;Wednesday&amp;#34;&lt;/span&gt;)
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="display:flex;"&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;span style="color:#66d9ef"&gt;case&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span style="color:#ae81ff"&gt;5&lt;/span&gt;:
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="display:flex;"&gt;&lt;span&gt; print(&lt;span style="color:#e6db74"&gt;&amp;#34;Thursday&amp;#34;&lt;/span&gt;)
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="display:flex;"&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;span style="color:#66d9ef"&gt;case&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span style="color:#ae81ff"&gt;6&lt;/span&gt;:
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="display:flex;"&gt;&lt;span&gt; print(&lt;span style="color:#e6db74"&gt;&amp;#34;Friday&amp;#34;&lt;/span&gt;)
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="display:flex;"&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;span style="color:#66d9ef"&gt;case&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span style="color:#ae81ff"&gt;7&lt;/span&gt;:
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="display:flex;"&gt;&lt;span&gt; print(&lt;span style="color:#e6db74"&gt;&amp;#34;Saturday&amp;#34;&lt;/span&gt;)
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="display:flex;"&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;span style="color:#66d9ef"&gt;default&lt;/span&gt;:
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="display:flex;"&gt;&lt;span&gt; print(&lt;span style="color:#e6db74"&gt;&amp;#34;Invalid day&amp;#34;&lt;/span&gt;)
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="display:flex;"&gt;&lt;span&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Please note:&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</description></item><item><title>4.4. Combining operators</title><link>/docs/02-swift-fundamentals/conditionals/4.4.-combining-operators/</link><pubDate>Mon, 01 Jan 0001 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><guid>/docs/02-swift-fundamentals/conditionals/4.4.-combining-operators/</guid><description>&lt;h1 id="44-combining-operators"&gt;4.4. Combining operators&lt;a class="anchor" href="#44-combining-operators"&gt;#&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/h1&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Swift has two operators that let us combine multiple conditions. They are &lt;code&gt;&amp;amp;&amp;amp;&lt;/code&gt; (&lt;code&gt;and&lt;/code&gt;), and &lt;code&gt;||&lt;/code&gt; (or).&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Let&amp;rsquo;s look into an example:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;div class="highlight"&gt;&lt;pre tabindex="0" style="color:#f8f8f2;background-color:#272822;-moz-tab-size:4;-o-tab-size:4;tab-size:4;-webkit-text-size-adjust:none;"&gt;&lt;code class="language-swift" data-lang="swift"&gt;&lt;span style="display:flex;"&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;span style="color:#66d9ef"&gt;let&lt;/span&gt; myAge = &lt;span style="color:#ae81ff"&gt;24&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="display:flex;"&gt;&lt;span&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="display:flex;"&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;span style="color:#66d9ef"&gt;if&lt;/span&gt; myAge &lt;span style="color:#f92672"&gt;&amp;gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span style="color:#ae81ff"&gt;18&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span style="color:#f92672"&gt;&amp;amp;&amp;amp;&lt;/span&gt; myAge &lt;span style="color:#f92672"&gt;&amp;lt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span style="color:#ae81ff"&gt;65&lt;/span&gt; {
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="display:flex;"&gt;&lt;span&gt; print(&lt;span style="color:#e6db74"&gt;&amp;#34;Eligible for the user study!&amp;#34;&lt;/span&gt;)
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="display:flex;"&gt;&lt;span&gt;}
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="display:flex;"&gt;&lt;span&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="display:flex;"&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;span style="color:#66d9ef"&gt;if&lt;/span&gt; myAge &lt;span style="color:#f92672"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span style="color:#ae81ff"&gt;18&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span style="color:#f92672"&gt;||&lt;/span&gt; myAge &lt;span style="color:#f92672"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span style="color:#ae81ff"&gt;65&lt;/span&gt; {
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="display:flex;"&gt;&lt;span&gt; print(&lt;span style="color:#e6db74"&gt;&amp;#34;Not eligible!&amp;#34;&lt;/span&gt;)
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="display:flex;"&gt;&lt;span&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;p&gt;It means if &lt;code&gt;myAge&lt;/code&gt; is more than or equal to 18 &lt;code&gt;and&lt;/code&gt; less than or equal to 65, print &amp;ldquo;Eligible for the user study!&amp;rdquo;; if &lt;code&gt;myAge&lt;/code&gt; is less than 18 &lt;code&gt;or&lt;/code&gt; greater than 65, print &amp;ldquo;Not eligible!&amp;rdquo;&lt;/p&gt;</description></item><item><title>4.5. Range operators</title><link>/docs/02-swift-fundamentals/conditionals/4.5.-range-operators/</link><pubDate>Mon, 01 Jan 0001 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><guid>/docs/02-swift-fundamentals/conditionals/4.5.-range-operators/</guid><description>&lt;h1 id="45-range-operators"&gt;4.5. Range operators&lt;a class="anchor" href="#45-range-operators"&gt;#&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/h1&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Swift can even represent a range of values. There are two kinds of range operators in Swift:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Closed range operators (&lt;code&gt;...&lt;/code&gt;) creates ranges up to and including the final value.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Half-open range operators (&lt;code&gt;..&amp;lt;&lt;/code&gt;) creates ranges up to and excluding the final value.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;For example, the range &lt;code&gt;1..5&lt;/code&gt; contains the numbers 1, 2, 3, 4, and 5. In contrast, the range &lt;code&gt;1..&amp;lt;5&lt;/code&gt; contains the numbers 1, 2, 3, and 4.&lt;/p&gt;</description></item><item><title>4.6. Reference Code</title><link>/docs/02-swift-fundamentals/conditionals/4.6.-reference-code/</link><pubDate>Mon, 01 Jan 0001 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><guid>/docs/02-swift-fundamentals/conditionals/4.6.-reference-code/</guid><description>&lt;h1 id="46-reference-code"&gt;4.6. Reference Code&lt;a class="anchor" href="#46-reference-code"&gt;#&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/h1&gt;
